# Cache rules for the Pages deploy. Read by Cloudflare from the root of what
# gets published; the site has no build step, so this file's home is the repo
# root and nothing has to copy it anywhere. The file itself is consumed at
# deploy time and never served.
#
# The default was giving style.css `public, max-age=14400` while index.html got
# `max-age=0, must-revalidate`. That pairing is the worst of both: the browser
# re-fetches the markup on every load and then styles it with a stylesheet up
# to four hours old, so a pushed CSS change looks like a deploy that silently
# failed. The markup and the stylesheet that dresses it should expire together.
#
# max-age=0 is a revalidation, not a re-download — the browser still sends the
# ETag and still gets a 304 with an empty body when nothing changed. What it
# buys is that a change is never more than one round trip away. On 36kB of CSS
# and 20kB of JS that trip is not worth defending. Images and fonts keep the
# long default: they are replaced by adding a file, not by editing one.
#
# Spelled out a path at a time rather than as /*.css — Pages matches a splat as
# a trailing wildcard, so an extension pattern quietly matches nothing and the
# rule looks applied while doing nothing at all. There are four such files.

/style.css
  cache-control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

/main.js
  cache-control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

/blog/style.css
  cache-control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

/blog/main.js
  cache-control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
